173:The owner answered every row of the 2026-11-05 decision page, in the terminal. The rows that revise a recorded rule were answered one by one: Q5.1/Q5.4, Q6.4, Q2.8, Q9.15 with P3, Q7.11 and Q4.7. Q3.12, Q9.19, Q7.10, Q1.6, P2, P4, P9, P13, P16 and P17a/b were also answered directly. The remaining Core rows were accepted as recommended, and the remaining project rows were approved on the owner's condition that Claude and Codex agree and that each is the best long-term design (both confirmed; every row carries Codex agreement). The full rows (question, options, reason, recommendation, agreement, revision and notes) are in [core_rows.json](archive/decisions-2026-11-05/core_rows.json) and [other_rows.json](archive/decisions-2026-11-05/other_rows.json); the concrete rules behind them are in [R4_RATIONALE.md](archive/decisions-2026-11-05/R4_RATIONALE.md), and where R4 and a row differ, the row governs.
179:- **OD45 and OD87** (rows Q5.1, Q5.4): capability arguments are written explicitly at every polymorphic call and checked against the callee; there is no capability inference and no inference solver. OD46 (type arguments from checked argument types) is unchanged.
232:- **Q5.1** Should capability arguments ever be inferred? — Explicit capability arguments on every polymorphic call. *Revises or adds: OD45, OD87.*
233:- **Q5.2** May explicit arguments contain the caller’s capability variables? — Explicit symbolic capability expressions and union.
234:- **Q5.3** What is capability-set equality and normal form? — Canonical semantic equality; refuse repeated names.
235:- **Q5.4** Is a capability inference solver still normative? — Explicit substitution checking; no inference solver. *Revises or adds: OD45, OD87.*
306:- **P23** Precedence between decisions, rows and drafts — Recorded decisions govern. An accepted row supersedes a recorded rule only through an explicitly identified, owner-approved revision (Q5.1/Q5.4 for OD45/OD87, Q6.4 for TYPE-22, Q2.8 for TRAIT-1, Q9.15/P3 for generated chapters). R4's replacements govern conflicting draft recommendations. Before your acceptance is recorded, every passage R4 F18 lists is reconciled (capability inference, generated chapters, roadmap authority, assurance wording, restart order and retention, Phase E, freeze dependencies, superseded Core recommendations), and historical conflicting text is marked superseded.
1160:| OD45 | <a id="od45"></a>Capability-variable inference (added 2026-10-29, from bug 063's ruling). Main today binds a callee's `cap C` variable from the capability set of an argument's function type when its type peek (`PeekType`) can compute that type without checking the argument, and refuses a variable no argument determines (E0242, `pick_generic::<i64>()`). Options: (a) specify the current compiler's peek-based rule, listing the argument forms it covers; (b) bind from the checked type of every argument, whatever its form; (c) require an explicit capability argument whenever an argument is not a name. Recommendation: (b), so whether a call infers does not depend on the argument's spelling Reviewed pick (2026-10-30, a recommendation that supersedes the recommendation above where they differ): (b) bind from the checked type of every argument; refuse unresolved or inconsistent ones; no result-context inference | the current compiler is (a) without the list | TRUST-4, TYPE-1; bug 063's record | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Bind from checked type of every argument; no result-context. **Revised by the owner 2026-11-05 (row Q5.1/Q5.4, [revision](#decided-2026-11-05-revisions)): capability arguments are written explicitly at every polymorphic call and checked; no capability inference** |
1202:| OD87 | <a id="od87"></a>Capability arguments in an expression turbofish (added 2026-10-30, item R2/R3 of the owner picks of that day). Question: Whether capability arguments (`T + C`, `{}`) may be written in an expression or method turbofish. Options: (a) the grammar narrows to type arguments there until a workload needs them; (b) the parser adds them. Pick (the coordinator's pick after the Kimi, Codex and Grok review of 2026-10-30, a recommendation and not a decision): (a) narrow. Austral: not applicable | the parser refuses them; the grammar derives them | SYN-1, TRUST-4, OPEN-45 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Accept explicit capability arguments in a turbofish (inference fills omitted ones). **Revised by the owner 2026-11-05 (row Q5.1/Q5.4, [revision](#decided-2026-11-05-revisions)): explicit capability arguments are required; inference no longer fills omitted ones** |
1207:| OD92 | <a id="od92"></a>A capability set inferred from callbacks (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing CALLABLE_VALUES and HANDLE_CAPABILITIES). Question: Whether a combinator may write its capability set as that of its callbacks (`with(f)`), the checker computing the union of the sets of the callbacks its body calls. Bears on TYPE-D1, TYPE-17, TRUST-4. The current compiler: no such form; `with(f)` reads `f` as a capability name (an unresolved one, which MOD-1 refuses and the current compiler accepts: bug 337), so a combinator names a capability parameter (`fn map<T, U, cap C>(.., f: fn(&T) with(C) -> U) with(C)`). The callable-values design (2026-06-12, [CALLABLE_VALUES_AND_CAPABILITIES.md](../legacy/docs/archive/design-records/CALLABLE_VALUES_AND_CAPABILITIES.md) §6) recorded the shortcut as specified and not built, after [research/language/capability-polymorphism.md](../legacy/research/language/capability-polymorphism.md); no roadmap item tracks it. Options: (a) no shortcut, combinators name capability parameters; (b) add it, as set propagation and not row unification. Austral: not applicable (capabilities are values). Pick: none given (raised by the absorption of 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-92 | see OPEN-92 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): No shortcut; name a capability parameter |
1208:| OD93 | <a id="od93"></a>Widening a function value's capability set (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing CALLABLE_VALUES and HANDLE_CAPABILITIES). Question: Whether a function value converts to a function type whose capability set contains its own, implicitly by subset or by an explicit conversion. Bears on TYPE-4, TYPE-17, TRUST-4. The current compiler: refused (E0220): `fn(Int) -> Int` is not a `fn(Int) with(Console) -> Int`, nor `with(File)` a `with(Std)`, and there is no explicit conversion; a capability-polymorphic parameter accepts either by instantiating its variable. The callable-values design (§10) says there is no implicit widening, and that "widening is explicit or via lattice subsumption only", with a lattice example (`Concurrent ⊇ Async`) whose names are not capabilities; HANDLE_CAPABILITIES R7 keeps handle types invariant, as TYPE-4 does. Options: (a) exact types, as in the current compiler; (b) subsumption into a larger set; (c) an explicit conversion. Austral: not applicable (capabilities are values). Pick: none given (raised by the absorption of 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-93 | see OPEN-93 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Exact types **Canonical current capability-set rule: authority rule 4; this earlier exact-types answer refers to it.** |
1223:| OD108 | <a id="od108"></a>A capability listed twice in a set (added 2026-10-30, from the Codex and Kimi review of the absorption). Question: Whether a capability set that lists one capability twice (`with(Console, Console)`) is the same set as the one that lists it once, is refused, or is a different set. Bears on TYPE-D1, TYPE-17, TYPE-4. The current compiler: accepted where it is written, and a different type: `fn(Int) with(Console) -> Int` is not a `fn(Int) with(Console, Console) -> Int`, in either direction (E0220), because `CapSet.normalize` sorts a set and does not remove a repeated name. The callable-values design ([CALLABLE_VALUES_AND_CAPABILITIES.md](../legacy/docs/archive/design-records/CALLABLE_VALUES_AND_CAPABILITIES.md)) calls the capabilities of a function type a set. Options: (a) the same set; (b) refuse a repeated capability where it is written; (c) a different set, as in the current compiler. Austral: not applicable (capabilities are values). Pick: none given (raised by the Codex and Kimi review of the absorption, 2026-10-30). | accepted, and a different set: `with(Console, Console)` is not `with(Console)` (E0220) | TYPE-D1, TYPE-17, TYPE-4 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Refuse a repeated capability |
