1208:| OD93 | <a id="od93"></a>Widening a function value's capability set (added 2026-10-30, raised while absorbing CALLABLE_VALUES and HANDLE_CAPABILITIES). Question: Whether a function value converts to a function type whose capability set contains its own, implicitly by subset or by an explicit conversion. Bears on TYPE-4, TYPE-17, TRUST-4. The current compiler: refused (E0220): `fn(Int) -> Int` is not a `fn(Int) with(Console) -> Int`, nor `with(File)` a `with(Std)`, and there is no explicit conversion; a capability-polymorphic parameter accepts either by instantiating its variable. The callable-values design (§10) says there is no implicit widening, and that "widening is explicit or via lattice subsumption only", with a lattice example (`Concurrent ⊇ Async`) whose names are not capabilities; HANDLE_CAPABILITIES R7 keeps handle types invariant, as TYPE-4 does. Options: (a) exact types, as in the current compiler; (b) subsumption into a larger set; (c) an explicit conversion. Austral: not applicable (capabilities are values). Pick: none given (raised by the absorption of 2026-10-30). | see OPEN-93 | see OPEN-93 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Exact types **Canonical current capability-set rule: authority rule 4; this earlier exact-types answer refers to it.** |
1223:| OD108 | <a id="od108"></a>A capability listed twice in a set (added 2026-10-30, from the Codex and Kimi review of the absorption). Question: Whether a capability set that lists one capability twice (`with(Console, Console)`) is the same set as the one that lists it once, is refused, or is a different set. Bears on TYPE-D1, TYPE-17, TYPE-4. The current compiler: accepted where it is written, and a different type: `fn(Int) with(Console) -> Int` is not a `fn(Int) with(Console, Console) -> Int`, in either direction (E0220), because `CapSet.normalize` sorts a set and does not remove a repeated name. The callable-values design ([CALLABLE_VALUES_AND_CAPABILITIES.md](../legacy/docs/archive/design-records/CALLABLE_VALUES_AND_CAPABILITIES.md)) calls the capabilities of a function type a set. Options: (a) the same set; (b) refuse a repeated capability where it is written; (c) a different set, as in the current compiler. Austral: not applicable (capabilities are values). Pick: none given (raised by the Codex and Kimi review of the absorption, 2026-10-30). | accepted, and a different set: `with(Console, Console)` is not `with(Console)` (E0220) | TYPE-D1, TYPE-17, TYPE-4 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Refuse a repeated capability |
1271:| OD156 | <a id="od156"></a>The capability of `get_args` (added 2026-10-31, raised while reviewing the builtins catalog of spec readability 2). Question: Which capability the builtin `get_args` requires, or whether the builtin is removed. Bears on TRUST-4, STMT-7, TRUST-9 and OPEN-136. The current compiler: Check gives `get_args()` the result `HeapArray<String>` and requires `Process` (E0520 with `Env` only); the builtin has no implementation on either consumer (bug 347). STMT-7's rationale says command-line arguments come from `std.args` with `Env`, and OD8 gives `main` the root resource value (TRUST-9). Options: (a) `Env`, as `std.args` requires; (b) `Process`, as the current compiler's signature says; (c) another capability, or none; (d) remove the builtin, and command-line arguments come from `std.args`. Austral: not checked. Pick: none given (raised while reviewing the builtins catalog of spec readability 2, 2026-10-31). | see OPEN-156 | see OPEN-156 | decided 2026-11-01 by the owner (final set): Superseded by the authority decision of 2026-11-01: `get_args` requires `with(Env)`, and `with(Alloc)` when it allocates. Revised 2026-11-02 by the owner's rule: it also takes the `Environment` grant, and an allocator when it allocates ([decisions](decisions.md#decided-2026-11-02-filesystem)) |
