## Comparison

Counts below are physical signature lines as printed, excluding "'`{` bodies, and value parameters (not generic/brand parameters). They measure these applications, not a general language benchmark. All signatures except retry_visit fit one line in both versions; automatic wrapping would change line counts without changing complexity.

| Signature | TODAY lines / value parameters | MERGED lines / value parameters | Other signature burden removed |
|---|---:|---:|---|
| server serve_one | 1 / 3 | 1 / 3 | three permission names |
| server main | 1 / 3 | 1 / 3 | three permission names |
| KV get | 1 / 2 | 1 / 2 | none |
| KV put | 1 / 4 | 1 / 4 | Alloc |
| KV delete | 1 / 2 | 1 / 2 | none |
| KV persist | 1 / 1 | 1 / 1 | Fs |
| KV close_store | 1 / 1 | 1 / 1 | Fs, Alloc |
| KV main | 1 / 2 | 1 / 2 | Fs, Alloc |
| retry_visit | 2 / 4 | 2 / 4 | one cap parameter; E on callback type and helper |
| announce | 1 / 2 | 1 / 2 | Console, Time |
| visit_three | 1 / 1 | 1 / 1 | Console, Time; three call-site set arguments in body |
| callback main | 1 / 2 | 1 / 2 | Console, Time |

Both have 13 physical signature lines and 27 value parameters across the 12 definitions. MERGED does not remove an allocator, directory, clock or stream parameter. A truly resource-free permission operation would replace a TODAY permission name with a MERGED token parameter, increasing value plumbing instead. Hiding parameters in a context is possible in both models and is not a fair count reduction attributable to the merge.

| Question | TODAY | MERGED |
|---|---|---|
| What does a signature say? | A directly readable, independently checked category ceiling plus resource inputs. It does not say which declared effects actually occur or reveal runtime grant scopes. A returned resource still needs permission to use. | The inputs, including nested owned capabilities and callback contexts, bound available authority. No independent category ceiling. Opaque types require checked interface summaries. A returned capability can increase the recipient'"'s authority. |
| “Can X reach the network?” | No Network in a concrete checked signature rules out ordinary network operations under the primitive/FFI premises, even when X receives a Socket hidden inside a struct. If Network is present, inspect reachable grants, call paths and scopes in facts; permission alone is insufficient. Generic signatures need instantiated sets. | Trace capability reachability through inputs, fields, acquisition and callback targets in facts. No reachable network capability rules it out only with complete constructor/foreign coverage. Presence establishes availability, not that an operation will occur. Scope may need dynamic values; unknown is not “no.” |
| Spec size and proof burden | Separate set syntax, semantic equality, subset/substitution checks, exact callback sets and value provenance. The small Lean authority part already has executable checking and containment proofs, but excludes much of resource safety. | Remove set-specific syntax/judgments/lemmas; retain ownership, identity, provenance, scope and foreign assumptions. Add a full value-flow/derivation containment theorem and sound, complete-enough summary rules. Net reduction is plausible, not measured. Merely deleting the set model produces no proof of object-capability safety. |
| Checker complexity | Extra parametric set inclusion and explicit argument checking; no inference solver since Q5. Set checks provide local errors when a function exceeds its declared ceiling. | Fewer type forms and no set checking. Existing ownership checking handles transfer, but sealed introduction, aggregate reachability, callback summaries and derived facts must cover every route. A forgotten resource in a broad context may be an audit/policy finding instead of a type error. |
| Generics and callbacks | Explicit "'`cap E`, exact callback types, explicit instantiation. No widening or `with(f)` escape hatch. Caller and callback authority remain visible independently of Ctx. | Ordinary generic Ctx and exact ordinary function types. Less ceremony and no artificial set mismatch. Authority visibility can move behind an abstract context; exported summaries must follow instantiation and indirect calls. |
| Failure modes | Permission omitted: compilation refuses, sometimes repetitive propagation. Broad declared sets reduce usefulness. Resource scope can still be too broad. FFI lies, incomplete primitive tables and wrong cleanup contracts undermine the claim. | Passing an overpowered aggregate grants more than intended. Returning a handle also returns authority. Accidental use of an already-held capability needs no signature edit. Forged tokens, hidden FFI state or incomplete reachability summaries break containment. Narrow at boundaries and inspect authority diffs. |
| Linear ownership | Distinguishes “owns it” from “may operate on it”; a custodian can hold a resource without operational permission. Both permissions and explicit cleanup need propagation. | Transfer and authority use one value discipline; custodian-only APIs need sealed wrappers or a restricted interface, not an effect ceiling. A sealed wrapper must expose no accessor that recovers authority. Zero-sized tokens still need linear accounting. |
| Second-class references | Long-lived contexts own resources; passing a borrowed context is temporary. Root-based exclusivity and container-not-in-context restrictions apply. | Exactly the same restrictions. Owning capabilities is natural, but owning an allocator alongside its dependents still needs brands/dependency summaries. Values-only does not create reference fields or reusable borrowed closures. |
| Purity, Secret and failures | Empty with is only an authority statement. Noninterference, declassification, constant time, failure and termination remain separate. | No capability inputs is only an authority statement. The same separate claims remain; owning an output capability never licenses secret disclosure. |
| Runtime cost | Permissions erased; real grants and dynamic scope checks remain. | Most resource representations can be identical; pure tokens can erase. No promised speedup: ownership, release metadata, dynamic confinement and native adapters still exist. |

### What happens to C5 and the Lean theorems?

Treat “C5 authority invariant” here as the authority-containment obligation, not a theorem named C5 in the current Lean file. The concrete proofs are [Proofs/Authority.lean](../ConcreteSpec/Proofs/Authority.lean): `sub_sound`, `sub_iff`, `mem_subst`, `equiv_iff`, `exec_contained`, and `authority_contained`. [The spec](../ConcreteSpec/Spec/Authority.lean) defines `CapExpr`, `PrimOk`, `ArgsOk`, `FnOk`, `ProgOk` and execution with erased sets.

TODAY `authority_contained` gives both sides: each observable operation uses a grant supplied to main and its capability belongs to main'"'s declared set. It covers finite prefixes, including incomplete execution; it needs no termination premise. Its grant environment currently passes unchanged identities through direct calls. It does not prove narrowed rights, freshness, linear closing or wrapper integrity.

Under MERGED, delete the capability-set conjunct, CapExpr machinery, capability substitution/equality lemmas and set obligations in PrimOk/ArgsOk/ProgOk. Ordinary argument/type/ownership checks stay. "'`exec_contained` and `authority_contained` must be replaced or generalized, not simply deleted: every event must act on a live capability rooted in startup authority through a valid acquisition/attenuation chain, with rights within that chain'"'s scope. The proof must handle returned and stored values, moves, loans, acquired children, callback contexts and partial traces. Runtime events and launcher refusal remain in [Observation.lean](../ConcreteSpec/Spec/Observation.lean). Ownership safety, allocator dependency safety, noninterference and constant-time theorems do not go away. Authority fact soundness and absence-discovery completeness remain necessary in both designs.

Thus C5 loses its separate declared-permission invariant; it retains and strengthens the provenance/attenuation obligation required for the advertised values-only claim. Comparing the line count of today's small direct-call set proof with an unspecified full object-capability theorem would be misleading.

## Decided text a merge would revise

The left column gives the operative old wording (short quotations or explicitly labelled summaries). The right column is proposed replacement text, not an owner answer. Shared Q7 contracts are deliberately retained rather than silently reopened.

| Decided location | Old text | Proposed new text |
|---|---|---|
| [Authority preamble and rules 1–2](../docs/decisions.md#decided-2026-11-02-authority) | “A value is not a permission, and a permission is not a value.” “Every operation needs both a resource value and a permission.” | Authority is carried only by sealed capability values. An operation needs a live borrowed or owned capability of the required type and rights; no with clause exists. |
| Authority rule 3 | Summary: capability variables range over sets; check exact instantiations parametrically. | Remove capability variables and set instantiation. Check ordinary generic bodies, capability-bearing values and exported authority summaries parametrically. |
| Authority rule 4; [OD92](../docs/decisions.md#od92), [OD93](../docs/decisions.md#od93) | Summary: exact function-pointer capability sets; callback callers declare those sets; contexts add no permission; no with(f). | Function pointers are capture-free ordinary function types. Callback authority comes through explicit value arguments/context; no effect-set parameter, widening or shortcut exists. Keep foreign function values refused. |
| Authority rule 5 | Summary: trusted and Unsafe never discharge operational capabilities; native behavior is an assumption. | Trusted and unsafe code may not bypass explicit capability operands or conceal ambient native effects. Native compliance with the capability contract is a named assumption. |
| Authority rules 6–7 | Summary: no umbrella capabilities; exhaustive primitive effect table; checked caller/callee inclusion. | No ambient authority or implicit context. The exhaustive primitive table specifies value inputs, rights, derivations, failures and observations. Composition checks value flow and derives authority summaries; remove set inclusion. Keep conservative blocking facts. |
| Authority rule 8 | “Returned values never supply permissions.” | Returned capability values transfer their authority, subject to the receiving ownership and scope rules. |
| Authority rule 9; Core Q2.1 | Summary: explicit effectful cleanup; Destroy needs at most Alloc; storage release/destruction/resource cleanup distinct. | Explicit cleanup consumes capability values with the same failure guarantees. Destruction uses release authority attached to owned storage, without separate Alloc permission. Keep the three cleanup categories distinct. |
| Authority rules 11–12; [OD38](../docs/decisions.md#od38) | Summary: unforgeable resources; main receives grants, declares permissions and consumes grants. | Unforgeable capability values arise only from startup or checked derivation/acquisition. main requests value parameters only, checked against target schema, and consumes all of them. No Root or permission set. |
| Authority rule 13; Q7.11 | Summary: separate reserved permission names, Console for standard streams and Device for devices. | Remove separate operational permission names. Standard-stream and device capability types/contracts distinguish their authority. Decide reservation cleanup explicitly; do not accidentally make formerly reserved names silently legal. |
| [Allocation](../docs/decisions.md#decided-2026-11-02-allocation); [OD83](../docs/decisions.md#od83); audit reset | Summary: Allocator<A> plus with(Alloc), including reset; no call-site allocator binding. | Allocator<A> supplies authority and identity; no Alloc clause or call-site binding. Preserve all Q1 brands, dependencies, reset freshness, metadata and failure contracts. |
| [Filesystem decision](../docs/decisions.md#decided-2026-11-02-filesystem); [OD156](../docs/decisions.md#od156) | Summary: service grant plus Fs/Env/Time/Network, allocator plus Alloc when allocating. | Each operation requires its explicit service capability and allocator when allocating; remove permission clauses. Preserve confined lookup, separate Resolver and all scopes. |
| [Q5.1–Q5.4](../docs/decisions.md#decided-2026-11-05-core); [OD45](../docs/decisions.md#od45), [OD87](../docs/decisions.md#od87), [OD108](../docs/decisions.md#od108) | Summary: explicit capability arguments, union, semantic set equality, duplicate refusal; no solver. | Retire capability-set syntax and all those obligations. Ordinary type inference and explicit brand arguments stay. No new authority inference solver is introduced. |
| Core Q7.8 | “Static authority checks plus atomic dynamic scope checks.” | Static capability typing, provenance and ownership checks plus atomic dynamic scope checks; remove the independent permission check. |
| Core Q7.10 and Q7.11 | Summary: approve target table with with(Console) for standard streams; missing grants exit 125. | Revise every target operation signature by deleting permission clauses. Keep stream capability operands, full table schema, close/narrow/acquire contracts, request/response observations and startup refusal 125. |
| [OD8](../docs/decisions.md#od8) revision and [facts vocabulary](../docs/decisions.md#decided-project-facts-report) | Summary: with plus resource values supersedes tokens-only OD8; facts separate declared authority and resources. | Record a new values-only revision, without restoring a Root or old token APIs. Facts report available and possibly used authority from value reachability, alongside resources, memory effects and evidence. |
| [Philosophy](../docs/PHILOSOPHY.md#mission-and-rules), authority admission criterion and refusals | Summary: effects appear in with; hiding operational permissions is refused; authority visible at use sites. | Authority must be explicit in capability value flow and findable from use sites through checked interface summaries. Refuse hidden authority; replace references to mandatory with clauses. This is an owner revision, not an editorial cleanup. |

Authority rules 10 and 14 need no substantive weakening. Q7.1–7.7 and Q7.9 retain their launch, scope, lifecycle and observation contracts; Q7.8/10/11 need the changes above. Secret/declassify does not need a new authority rule. Callable context modes, exact ordinary function typing, no captures and second-class references remain decided. Any entropy signatures added by the later randomness row need the same explicit reconciliation as the other primitive signatures. Generated spec chapters, typed summaries, policies and checker proofs would also need migration after an owner answer; none should be changed provisionally.

## Verdict and recommendation

Keep TODAY as the implementation baseline. MERGED is coherent and worth presenting, but these examples do not establish that it is the better long-term choice. Most runtime values and all lifecycle/error work already exist TODAY. Removing with primarily saves set machinery and callback annotations. It also removes a useful independent ceiling: the KV helpers can hold Store without Fs today; after merging, an implementation edit can start using the contained Directory without changing those signatures. That matters for audit review, even if derived facts expose the change afterward.

The strongest reason to merge is conceptual and formal economy: one authority-transfer mechanism integrated with linear ownership, with substantially cleaner generic callbacks. The strongest reason to keep the split is a small, local, programmer-declared ceiling that remains visible when types and contexts become opaque. Neither is inherently redundant: values choose the reachable things, while TODAY's permission set independently limits the operations. Pure tokens would be useful only for genuinely resource-free authority; adding them alongside every existing grant defeats the proposed simplification.

[Philosophy's conflict order and admission test](../docs/PHILOSOPHY.md#conflict-order) put correctness, auditability and explicit authority ahead of brevity, and require declaration-level summaries. On that basis I recommend no language revision yet. This is not a claim that set syntax must be retained forever, or that changes are bad: the promised proof/checker reduction has not been demonstrated against the obligations it would replace.

I would change my recommendation if a small formal prototype established attenuation and provenance for returned/stored capabilities and explicit callbacks, and a checked-summary prototype answered network reachability across opaque interfaces without whole-program body inspection. Measure both designs on these workloads with accurate native assumptions, incomplete-fact cases, authority diffs, and realistic broad-context mistakes. Show a meaningful reduction in specification/checker complexity and callback maintenance while preserving review visibility. Conversely, if keeping authority visible requires reintroducing programmer-written permission sets under another name, or opaque contexts routinely yield unknown facts, the case for MERGED weakens substantially. An owner may still prefer its single mechanism, but that tradeoff should be explicit.

